\section{BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK}
\label{sec:background}

This section summarizes why we study one-shot distillation with homomorphic-encryption-compatible students. The main issue is not only communication cost, but also what the server can infer from exchanged information. Iterative training protocols expose model updates over many rounds, while one-shot protocols reduce interaction but still expose transferred knowledge if that knowledge is sent in plaintext.

Prior work has explored several defenses, including differential privacy, secure aggregation, and homomorphic encryption. Each addresses part of the problem, but each also introduces practical trade-offs. Our work is positioned at the intersection of these lines: one-shot communication, encrypted target handling, and student architectures that are compatible with encrypted computation.

\subsection{The Multi-Round Attack Surface of FL and SFL}
Standard federated learning keeps raw data local but repeatedly exchanges model updates. Gradient inversion work shows that these updates can leak training information \cite{zhu2019deep}. Later analysis shows that, over many rounds, leakage risk remains even when secure aggregation is used \cite{so2023securing}.

Split federated learning reduces full-model exposure by exchanging intermediate representations. However, it still depends on repeated synchronization and can leak information through those intermediate states. This motivates one-shot settings where communication is limited to a single round \cite{wang2025towards}.

\subsection{The Sequential Bottlenecks and Leakage of Split Learning}
Split learning places different model parts on client and server. In multi-client settings, this usually creates sequential execution, so total runtime is sensitive to stragglers. It also creates additional leakage channels through exchanged activations and gradients.

Prior studies report attacks in this setting, including label leakage and feature reconstruction \cite{kariyappa2021gradient, xu2024stealthy}. These findings show that moving from full-model updates to intermediate representations does not remove privacy risk by itself.

\subsection{The Evolution and Vulnerability of One-Shot Distillation}
One-shot federated learning reduces repeated exposure by sending knowledge once. A practical challenge is how to provide useful distillation signals without sharing private data. This has led to synthetic-data and data-free approaches such as DENSE, FedDiff, and FedSD2C \cite{zhang2022dense, feddiff2024, chen2024fedsd2c}.

These methods are useful, but they do not automatically provide cryptographic protection of transferred knowledge. If logits, synthetic distillates, or related signals are visible in plaintext at the server, inference risk remains. Membership inference analysis on distillation settings supports this concern \cite{jagielski2023students}.

\subsection{The Fragility of Statistical Privacy and Sybil Attacks}
Local differential privacy can protect transmitted outputs, but one-shot distillation needs rich soft-label information. Stronger noise improves privacy and often reduces utility in distillation quality \cite{shao2023selective}. In addition, LDP aggregation can be sensitive to malicious participants; Sybil manipulation analysis provides formal bounds for this effect \cite{cheu2021manipulation}.

Secure aggregation and SMPC protect the communication channel, but the final aggregate can still be informative to a curious server in some settings \cite{kerkouche2023client}. These results motivate approaches where knowledge remains encrypted during server-side processing, not only during transport.

\subsection{The Intractability of Iterative HE-FL}
Homomorphic encryption is attractive because it provides cryptographic protection, but direct iterative HE-FL remains expensive for deep models. Prior measurements report large runtime and communication overheads compared to plaintext training \cite{zhang2020batchcrypt, jin2023fedml}. CKKS ciphertext expansion also increases bandwidth cost for iterative exchanges \cite{wei2025fedshe}.

This is one reason to focus on one-shot settings: communication happens once, and encrypted computation can be structured more carefully around the distillation objective.

\subsection{The Need for HE-Compatible Server-Side Student Training}
The remaining technical gap is stable student training when supervision signals are handled under HE constraints. HE supports addition and multiplication naturally, but standard distillation pipelines rely on operations such as softmax, ReLU, and batch normalization. This mismatch is widely recognized in HE learning literature \cite{agamennone2025polynomial, alhossain2025training}.

Our method addresses this gap by using HE-compatible student design and training choices: polynomial activations, ChannelScale normalization, MSE supervision instead of KL-softmax, and blockwise progressive stacking. CKKS rounding behavior across multiplicative levels is also considered in this design \cite{cheon2017ckks}. The result is a one-shot distillation framework where client knowledge is protected during server-side processing and the student remains compatible with encrypted computation.



