% % \section{BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK}

% % To establish a communication-efficient and cryptographically secure collaborative learning framework, the literature has explored numerous independent pathways---ranging from Split Learning and iterative Homomorphic Encryption to Differential Privacy. However, examining the concrete empirical limitations of each approach reveals why achieving scalable, privacy-guaranteed distillation inherently requires a convergence toward \textbf{One-Shot Homomorphic Encryption-compatible architectures}.

% % \subsection{The Vulnerability of Iterative FL and Constraints of Split Learning}
% % Standard Federated Learning (FL) mitigates direct data exposure by transmitting model updates. However, it introduces a severe and continuous attack surface. ``Deep Leakage from Gradients'' (DLG) fundamentally breaks the privacy premise of FL by demonstrating that shared gradients encode sufficient mathematical information to reconstruct local training inputs with pixel-wise accuracy \cite{zhu2019deep}. 

% % To address gradient leakage while maintaining encrypted server-side training, protocols such as Split Federated Learning (SFL) and Split Learning under Homomorphic Encryption (e.g., the CURE framework) have been proposed. SFL partitions the model, keeping raw data and initial layers local while the server computes the rest. Despite its privacy benefits, SFL introduces critical architectural bottlenecks. First, its sequential nature creates a massive straggler dependency; recent analyses in MU-SplitFed demonstrate that the synchronization requirement forces the server to wait for individual clients, causing total delay to scale strictly with the slowest participant \cite{wu2025musplitfed}. Second, the communication overhead is severe: empirical benchmarks of U-shaped Split Learning combined with HE reveal a 43.9\% increase in training latency per epoch (8.56s vs.\ 4.8s locally) due to the continuous round-trip transmission of intermediate activations \cite{splitways2023}.

% % Furthermore, exchanging intermediate activations is not chemically secure. Cryptographic protections notwithstanding, frameworks relying on split architectures remain vulnerable to Feature Space Hijacking Attacks (FSHA) and Gradient Inversion Attacks, the latter having been shown to leak a client's private labels with up to 97.01\% accuracy \cite{fsha2022, gradientinversion2024}. Therefore, the sequential latency and residual activation leakage of SFL render it impractical for scalable, privacy-guaranteed deployment.

% % \subsection{The Intractability of Multi-Round HE-FL}
