% % \subsection{The Need for HE-Compatible Server-Side Student Training}
% % To achieve cryptographically guaranteed privacy without destroying dark knowledge via LDP or exposing aggregates via SMPC, the distillation targets (logits) must remain encrypted \textit{during both aggregation and student training}.

% % This presents a profound technical challenge: HE schemes natively support only addition and multiplication. Training a student network using standard distillation requires non-polynomial non-linearities, including Softmax (for KL divergence), ReLU activations, and Batch Normalization. As noted in recent literature, ``a major limitation in homomorphic encryption is the inability to efficiently handle non-linear activation functions... due to their non-polynomial nature'' \cite{ieice2025he}.

% % Currently, the literature predominantly addresses this by focusing on encrypted \textit{inference}---where a pre-trained model evaluates encrypted data---or by encrypting \textit{model parameters} for simple averaging \cite{ibm_he_inference2021, he_parameters2023}. Attempts to train Polynomial Neural Networks (PNNs) directly over encrypted data frequently encounter numerical instability and gradient explosion \cite{pnn_instability2025}. Consequently, the specific challenge of executing a complete server-side backpropagation pass over encrypted distillation targets remains unaddressed. 

% % This gap directly motivates the core contribution of OP-FD. By explicitly designing HE-compatible student architectures---utilizing Goldschmidt-approximated Batch Normalization, learnable polynomial activations, and Chebyshev-approximated KL divergence (PolyKL)---our framework enables stable, one-shot server-side training where the central server learns from cryptographically secure distillation targets without ever requiring access to plaintext data.



